# Ninety-odd settings rows are more than a section strip and a
# scrollbar can answer for, so `/` opens the family filter pill under
# the box's title and the list narrows live — over each row's label,
# the word its current value reads as, and its section's name.
#
# The strip stays put under the pill (a section with no match dims but
# is still there to click), the footer swaps its scroll position for
# `N of M`, and Esc takes the query before it takes the box.

command view.settings
expect screen contains "UI · Editor · AI · Integrations · Reset"
expect screen contains "▸ Line numbers:"

# `/` opens the pill; typing narrows the rows as it goes.
key /
expect screen contains "type to filter"
type dock
expect screen contains "󰍉 dock"
expect screen contains "── UI ──"
expect screen contains "Launcher dock:"
expect screen contains "Launcher dock edge:"
expect screen contains "Bottom dock at start:"
# Everything else is gone, headers included — `Line numbers` is three
# rows above `Launcher dock` unfiltered.
expect screen lacks "Line numbers"
expect screen lacks "── Editor ──"
expect screen lacks "── AI ──"
# The footer counts what the query left. The total moves as rows are
# added, so the assertion is the form, not the number.
expect screen contains " of "

# The box does not shrink to fit what the query left. At 120x40 it is
# the height the whole list asks for (the 70 % cap), whether five rows
# are showing or ninety — otherwise the box would jump and re-centre
# with every character, sliding the matches out from under the pointer
# about to click one. Row 30 is empty overlay ground well below the
# last match, and row 34 is outside the frame: `expect color` is the
# only way a script sees WHERE the box is, because a `contains` finds
# the border wherever it moved to.
expect color 60 30 bg #353b45
expect color 60 34 bg not #353b45

# Enter hands the list back to the cursor, query and all — and the
# cursor is on a match, so `←` adjusts the row the search found.
key enter
expect screen contains "󰍉 dock"
expect screen contains "▸ Launcher dock:"
expect screen contains "always / [auto_hide] / hidden"
key left
expect screen contains "[always] / auto_hide / hidden"

# Esc takes the query and puts the whole list back; the box stays.
key esc
expect screen lacks "󰍉 dock"
expect screen contains "▸ Launcher dock:"
expect screen contains "Line numbers"

# The second Esc is the box's own way out — and it cancels, so the
# value the filter found goes back to what it was.
key esc
expect screen lacks "UI · Editor · AI · Integrations · Reset"
command view.settings
expect screen contains "Launcher dock:"
expect screen contains "always / [auto_hide] / hidden"
key esc

# A query with exactly one answer: the count form, both halves of it.
command view.settings
key /
type rainbow
expect screen contains "Rainbow brackets:"
expect screen contains "1 of "
expect screen lacks "Line numbers"
key esc
key esc

# `view.settings_search` is the palette's way in: the box opens with the
# pill already holding the keys, so `ctrl+shift+p` → "Settings: search"
# → a query is one move. (`/` inside the box does the same, but a user
# has to already be in the box to learn that.)
#
# The query it answers is `fold` — the Editor section's fold-arrow row,
# which sits far below the fold of the first window and is reached here
# by searching rather than by scrolling to a row number that moves.
command view.settings_search
expect screen contains "type to filter"
type fold
expect screen contains "󰍉 fold"
expect screen contains "── Editor ──"
expect screen contains "Always show fold arrows:"
expect screen contains "[off] / on"
expect screen lacks "Line numbers"
# The cursor is on the match, so `←` adjusts the row the search found.
key enter
expect screen contains "▸ Always show fold arrows:"
key left
expect screen contains "off / [on]"
key esc
key esc

# A query nothing answers to says so, rather than leaving a blank box.
command view.settings
key /
type zzzz
expect screen contains "no setting matches \"zzzz\""
expect screen lacks "▸ Line numbers:"
key esc
expect screen contains "▸ Line numbers:"
key esc
